---

# 17. Sorting

Allow:

```text
Created Date
Updated Date
Name
Revenue
Orders
Products
Status
```

Sorting must use deterministic backend ordering.

---

# 18. Pagination

Use server-side pagination.

Recommended API:

```text
GET /admin/stores?page=1&limit=25
```

Prefer cursor pagination when the existing platform supports it.

Never load the complete store dataset into the browser.

---

# 19. Store Actions

Available actions depend on lifecycle.

```text
View
Edit
View Storefront
Activate
Suspend
Enable Maintenance
Disable Maintenance
Archive
View Activity
```

Invalid actions must not be displayed or must be disabled.

Backend remains authoritative.

---

# 20. Bulk Actions

Allowed bulk operations:

```text
Activate
Suspend
Enable Maintenance
Disable Maintenance
Assign Store Group
Export
```

Dangerous actions require confirmation.

Example:

```text
Suspend 12 stores?

This may prevent new customer transactions.

[Cancel]
[Confirm]
```

---

# 21. Store Details Navigation

Selecting a store should navigate to:

```text
/admin/stores/:storeId
```

Store Details is defined separately in:

```text
store-details.mdc
```

Store Management must not duplicate the Store Details implementation.

---

# 22. RBAC

Recommended permissions:

```text
store.view
store.create
store.update
store.activate
store.suspend
store.maintenance
store.archive
store.domain.manage
store.staff.manage
store.audit.view
```

Permission evaluation:

```text
Authenticated User
        ↓
Platform RBAC
        ↓
Vendor Scope
        ↓
Store Scope
        ↓
Action Authorization
```

Frontend permissions are UX controls only.

Authorization must be enforced on the backend.

---

# 23. Vendor Isolation

A vendor must only access authorized stores.

Example:

```text
Vendor A
├── Store A1
└── Store A2

Vendor B
├── Store B1
└── Store B2
```

Vendor A must never retrieve:

```text
Store B1
Store B2
```

by manipulating:

```text
storeId
vendorId
query parameters
route parameters
request body
```

Protect against IDOR.

---

# 24. Platform Admin

Platform administrators may have platform-wide access depending on RBAC.

Platform-wide access must still be auditable.

Never bypass audit logging because the actor is an administrator.

---

# 25. Domain Management

Store Management may expose the domain configuration entry point.

Domain ownership remains in the domain/website infrastructure.

Support:

```text
Platform Subdomain
Custom Domain
Custom Subdomain
Redirect Domain
Primary Domain
```

Domain states:

```text
PENDING
VERIFYING
VERIFIED
FAILED
SUSPENDED
```

Rules:

* Primary domain must be unique.
* Domain ownership must be verified.
* One active domain cannot belong to multiple stores.
* Canonical URL must use the primary domain.
* Redirect domains must not create duplicate SEO pages.

---

# 26. Store Groups

Optional enterprise feature:

```text
Store Groups
```

Examples:

```text
Dhaka Stores
Chittagong Stores
Retail Stores
Outlet Stores
Premium Stores
Vendor A Stores
```

Groups are organizational constructs.

Do not make Store Groups the source of business-domain ownership.

---

# 27. Store Health

Store Management should expose a summarized health state.

```text
HEALTHY
WARNING
CRITICAL
```

Possible checks:

```text
Store Status
Domain
SSL
Catalog
Inventory
Payment
Shipping
Tax
POS
SEO
Notifications
```

Detailed health belongs to Store Details.

---

# 28. Store Management API

Recommended:

```text
GET    /admin/stores
POST   /admin/stores
GET    /admin/stores/:storeId
PATCH  /admin/stores/:storeId

POST   /admin/stores/:storeId/activate
POST   /admin/stores/:storeId/suspend
POST   /admin/stores/:storeId/maintenance
POST   /admin/stores/:storeId/archive

GET    /admin/stores/:storeId/health
GET    /admin/stores/:storeId/provisioning
GET    /admin/stores/:storeId/activity
```

Avoid generic:

```text
PATCH /admin/stores/:id/settings
```

for every business domain.

Delegate domain-specific configuration to the appropriate module.

---

# 29. Repository Boundary

Example:

```typescript
interface StoreRepository {
  findById(id: StoreId): Promise<Store | null>;

  findMany(query: StoreListQuery): Promise<Paginated<Store>>;

  findByCode(code: string): Promise<Store | null>;

  save(store: Store): Promise<void>;
}
```

Repositories must not expose unrelated domain persistence.

---

# 30. Store Domain Services

Recommended:

```text
StoreLifecycleService
StoreValidationService
StoreProvisioningService
StoreAuthorizationService
StoreHealthService
StoreDomainService
```

Keep domain logic out of controllers.

---

# 31. Error Handling

Use platform-standard errors.

Examples:

```text
STORE_NOT_FOUND
STORE_CODE_ALREADY_EXISTS
STORE_DOMAIN_ALREADY_EXISTS
INVALID_STORE_STATE
STORE_ACCESS_DENIED
INVALID_LIFECYCLE_TRANSITION
PROVISIONING_IN_PROGRESS
STORE_ARCHIVED
```

Do not leak database errors to the frontend.

---

# 32. Audit

Record:

```text
StoreCreated
StoreUpdated
StoreActivated
StoreSuspended
StoreMaintenanceEnabled
StoreArchived
DomainChanged
OwnershipChanged
StoreSettingsChanged
```

Include:

```text
actorId
storeId
vendorId
action
timestamp
before
after
correlationId
```

---

# 33. Performance

Store Management must support thousands or millions of store records.

Use:

* Server-side pagination
* Indexed search
* Indexed filters
* Backend aggregation
* Query projections
* Caching where appropriate
* Lazy loading

Do not perform N+1 queries.

---

# 34. Testing

Required:

```text
Store creation
Store listing
Search
Filtering
Sorting
Pagination
Lifecycle transitions
Duplicate store code
Duplicate domain
Vendor isolation
Store isolation
RBAC
Bulk operations
Provisioning state
Audit logging
```

Security tests must include IDOR attempts.

---

# 35. Cursor Implementation Rules

Before changing code:

1. Inspect existing Store module.
2. Inspect existing Admin UI.
3. Inspect existing RBAC.
4. Inspect existing table components.
5. Inspect existing filter components.
6. Inspect existing lifecycle implementation.
7. Inspect existing provisioning workflow.
8. Inspect existing audit infrastructure.
9. Inspect existing API conventions.
10. Inspect existing design system.

Then implement using existing patterns.

Do not rewrite unrelated modules.

Do not duplicate business domains.

Do not bypass domain services.

Do not create a second provisioning system.

Do not introduce a second RBAC system.

Do not introduce a second event bus.

---

# 36. Definition of Done

Store Management is complete when:

* Store list works.
* Search works.
* Filtering works.
* Sorting works.
* Pagination works.
* Store creation integrates with provisioning.
* Lifecycle controls work.
* Vendor isolation works.
* Store isolation works.
* RBAC works.
* Bulk operations are safe.
* Audit logging works.
* Store Details navigation works.
* Health status is available.
* Provisioning state is visible.
* Tests pass.
* No unrelated modules are duplicated.

